l":"https://ictnews.org/sports/from-the-2010-olympics-to-the-world-cup-vancouver-has-pioneered-indigenous-inclusion/","id":"summary","predicate":"summary","object":"Anne M. Peterson AP Soccer Writer VANCOUVER, British Columbia â€” Scattered among the many big screens airing World Cup matches and the food stands with local fare like poutine, Vancouverâ€™s fan festival includes exhibits recognizing the host cityâ€™s Indigenous communities: the Squamish, the Musqueam and Tsleil-Waututh nations. The First Nations have been involved in more than just the fan fest, however. They are partners with the local World Cup organizing committee, drawing on a tradition started at the 2010 Winter Olympics. Itâ€™s the latest example of how Indigenous inclusion has increasingly figured into big sporting events, from the 2023 Womenâ€™s World Cup in Australia and New Zealand to the 2028 Los Angeles Olympics. Vancouverâ€™s goal was to ensure that the First Nations were at the table in the planning process for the World Cup, and ultimately provide a platform that brings awareness to the cityâ€™s Indigenous communities and creates a lasting legacy. â€œWhat we found in the 2010 Games, and also now, is that one of the main messages is to share the diversity of culture that we have. Weâ€™re not all the same, and I think that also we donâ€™t just live in history books, weâ€™re living and...","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-18T04:08:29.665Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"from-the-2010-olympics-to-the-world-cup-vancouver-has-pioneered-indigenous-inclusion","sourceUrl":"https://ictnews.org/sports/from-the-2010-olympics-to-the-world-cup-vancouver-has-pioneered-indigenous-inclusion/","id":"published","predicate":"published","object":"Thu, 16 Jul 2026 10:00:00 +0000","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-18T04:08:29.665Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"meet-our-20